<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />

  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm">
        <a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-[#5d3a1d]">/</span>
        <span class="text-[#5d3a1d]">Klaviyo vs Mailchimp</span>
      </nav>

      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="catalog-kicker">Head-to-head</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Klaviyo vs Mailchimp for Shopify email tools</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">
          Mailchimp is the tool your team already knows. Klaviyo is the tool Shopify retention forums insist you need. This proposed evaluation compares both as full email-tool stacks — newsletters, lifecycle automation, and order-adjacent customer moments — using hypothetical fixtures: a specialty tea subscription shop, a climbing-gear retailer with long research cycles, and a vinyl record store using a legacy newsletter template. The question is not brand loyalty; it is whether your email layer matches how customers actually buy.
        </p>
      </header>

      <div class="mt-12 max-w-4xl">
        <TldrBox
          picks={[
            { label: 'Shopify lifecycle', tool: 'Klaviyo', reason: 'Cart, browse, replenishment, and flow revenue attribution built for ecommerce.' },
            { label: 'Familiar editor', tool: 'Mailchimp', reason: 'Low onboarding friction for teams with years of muscle memory.' },
            { label: 'Newsletters + sales', tool: 'Mailchimp', reason: 'Fast broadcast setup before lifecycle complexity arrives.' },
            { label: 'Retention at scale', tool: 'Klaviyo', reason: 'Segmentation and suppression that survive peak season.' },
            { label: 'Middle path', tool: 'Sequenzy', reason: 'Lifecycle playbooks if Klaviyo feels heavy and Mailchimp feels thin.' },
          ]}
        />
      </div>

      <div class="mt-12 card overflow-x-auto">
        <h2 class="px-5 py-4 font-serif text-2xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Comparison at a glance</h2>
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Klaviyo</th><th>Mailchimp</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Tool category</td><td>Shopify retention platform</td><td>General email marketing suite</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Newsletters</td><td>Strong, segment-aware</td><td>Excellent, template-rich</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Lifecycle automation</td><td>Advanced flows, native triggers</td><td>Customer Journeys, limited branching</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Shopify events</td><td>Browse, cart, orders, catalog</td><td>Orders, products, basic cart</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Billing</td><td>Active profiles + SMS</td><td>Contacts</td></tr>
            <tr><td>SMS</td><td>Native, coordinated</td><td>Available, peripheral</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Reporting</td><td>Revenue by flow and segment</td><td>Campaign metrics, weak flow GMV</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Non-email extras</td><td>Focused on retention</td><td>Landing pages, surveys, CRM-lite</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

                  <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Proposed vinyl record shop test: 350 monthly orders</h2>
        <p>
          Proposed comparison test for Klaviyo and Mailchimp: a hypothetical vinyl record shop doing roughly 350 monthly orders where rare pressings with two-hundred-unit runs would shape the retention calendar. The proposed constraint is lifecycle depth across browse cart replenishment and winback. For Klaviyo, test Shopify browse and order event coverage for collection targeting; a team without a retention engineer would need grading-guide PDF offers live before Record Store Day. For Mailchimp, verify the current marketing hub requirements, including landing pages, surveys and social integrations, then assess whether work involving VIP allocation drops can be maintained as a weekly merchandising task. Shopify email tools must cover newsletters, lifecycle automation, and transactional-style customer moments without collision; this pairing only works when signup tags, gift-order properties, and recent-purchaser suppressions sync at handoff.
        </p>
        <p>
          Use a separate hypothetical mechanical keyboard vendor fixture to test operational discipline; this is not a measured outcome for the preceding catalog. During GB close deadlines, test a documented send priority: transactional-style pre-order confirmations first, active cart recovery second, welcome and education third, promotional broadcasts last. Test exclusion visibility in each installed configuration. Compare current quotes for cold popup imports and broadcast-heavy months using matched annual profiles, sends, SMS, modules, support and operator labor; no billing penalty or platform winner has been observed. Maintain a cross-sender collision register; test gift-buyer exclusions and SMS/email touches forty-five minutes apart as failure fixtures. A delay does not establish channel or purpose permission. Recheck current purchase and suppression state at dispatch, assign one owner per job, cancel stale queues across all senders and deduplicate replays.
        </p>

